Modifications made for RimBoard: rewritten in Kotlin against Android's Canvas;
particle count derived from the view size at dp spacing rather than a fixed
30,000; positions held in one reused FloatArray instead of allocating an
ImageData per frame; the per-frame constants scaled to elapsed time so the
motion is the same at 60Hz and 120Hz; and the pointer replaced by the position
of the last key press, with no idle animation.

The "Night sky" live background (ui/Starfield.kt) is original to RimBoard.

Third-party data
----------------

Part of the bundled next-word prediction models
(all files under app/src/main/assets/predictions/) is derived from:

  Tatoeba sentence exports
  https://tatoeba.org

  Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 2.0 France
  (CC BY 2.0 FR): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/fr/

Modifications made for RimBoard: counted into word pairs, filtered to words
already present in the bundled dictionary for that language, and filtered
again to remove tokens over-represented in that corpus relative to the
dictionary — Tatoeba is a language-teaching collection whose sentences are
largely about two people named Tom and Mary, and without that filter "tom"
would be the commonest prediction in the language. See tools/build_ngrams.py.

CC BY 2.0 FR requires attribution and does not impose share-alike, so unlike
the dictionaries below these files carry no licence onward. The entries at the
head of each row are hand-written for RimBoard and are covered by its own
licence; the corpus-derived entries follow them.

The word-frequency dictionaries — both the bundled ones (all files
under app/src/main/assets/dictionaries/) and the extended ones the app
can download or import (the `dictionaries` branch of this repository,
built by tools/fetch_dictionaries.py --extended) — are derived from:

  FrequencyWords by Hermit Dave
  https://github.com/hermitdave/FrequencyWords

  Generated from the OpenSubtitles 2018 corpus
  (https://opus.nlpl.eu/OpenSubtitles.php).

  Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
  (CC BY-SA 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

Modifications made for RimBoard: filtered to the alphabetic words of each
language, deduplicated, and cut to —

  200,000 entries by frequency rank, for every language except English

  English, and the extended dictionaries for the thirteen languages that
  have one, instead keep every word the corpus recorded at least five
  times: 298,946 entries for English, and between 232,384 (German) and
  645,346 (Hungarian) for the rest

The two rules cross over. A rank cut adapts to whatever the corpus
holds; a count threshold does not, so where a corpus is thin the
200,000-entry list already reaches past five occurrences and there is no
extended list to offer. That is why eight languages have none.

Three lists have further per-language corrections, which leave them below
the figures above:

  Romanian — the legacy cedilla letters (s-cedilla U+015F, t-cedilla
  U+0163) are folded to the comma-below letters Romanian actually uses
  (U+0219, U+021B), with the frequencies of the two spellings summed.
  The corpus mixes both; only the comma-below pair is standard.

  Portuguese — the trema (u-diaeresis U+00FC) is folded away, again
  summing frequencies. It was abolished by the 1990 Orthographic
  Agreement, so "freqüência" is now "frequência".

  Italian — entries containing i-circumflex (U+00EE) are dropped. It is
  not modern Italian orthography, and every such entry was another
  language present in the corpus ("în" is Romanian, "maître" French).
  Dropped rather than folded, so no Italian word inherits another
  language's counts.

The filtering, folding and truncation are done by
tools/fetch_dictionaries.py, which is the authoritative description of
what was changed.

Under the CC BY-SA 4.0 terms, these dictionary files (and any derivative
dictionary files) remain licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.


